    "q-343282": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343282",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+1)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=6.$ What is $W(3)?$\n\n$W(3)=$ {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}(x+1)^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{1+1} \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{12}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =\\frac{12}{3+1}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
